import sys, io, re
hist = sys.argv[1]
h = io.open(hist, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
rows = [i for i, l in enumerate(h) if re.match(r'^\| \d+ \|', l)]
n = max(int(h[i].split('|')[1]) for i in rows) + 1
h.insert(rows[-1] + 1, f"| {n} | 2026-11-04 | **The song Lesson Book: Lesson 1 drafted, and an overview page (chat window, her 10:29 to 10:54).** Play-along (playalong-mary.html, an Artifact page): count-in, three speeds and her own speed (40 to 160, minus and plus, a number, a slider), a piano duet by default (the teacher part taken out of the book) or a band in three styles made from the chord letters (music box, boom-chick, ballad); the tune and one left-hand Way to switch on or off. An overview page of the book (overview.html, an Artifact): the frame, the fifteen songs with their rooms and chord stages, the lessons made with page pictures and PDFs. Lesson 1, Rain, Rain, Go Away (her 10:54: the first three lessons start from finger numbers and explain the very basics instead of ways; no staff from the start): the tune in two notes, G and E (the common version has an A on 'an-'; simplified, to check with her), drawn before the staff, high notes above one line and low below it; page 1 the counting and crotchet and minim, two bars a line, large; page 2 the hand (thumb on E, finger 3 on G) and the tune with a letter box under every note; page 3 'Hello, piano': finger numbers on two drawn hands, the black keys in twos and threes with every C, how to sit, then Play it, right hand and left hand (the numbers swap: the hands are mirrors), and the check. The generator now takes each lesson's text, chips, values, position and jobs from the song file (rain.json; early.py for the basics page). The rhyme: Wikipedia, Rain Rain Go Away (Howell's proverbs, 1659). |")
io.open(hist, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(h)); print('row', n)
